WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.110 --> 00:00:05.070
For the past year, we’ve looked at Apple’s
base 13” MacBook Pro as a pretty bad value

00:00:05.070 --> 00:00:10.309
compared to Apple’s newly updated MacBook
Air and the more expensive $1800 Touch Bar

00:00:10.309 --> 00:00:15.030
Pro, since it packed a pretty slow dual-core
processor and was lacking a few features,

00:00:15.030 --> 00:00:19.810
but with Apple’s new $1300 base MacBook
Pro, we’ve completely changed our mind,

00:00:19.810 --> 00:00:22.610
and we’re now calling it the best value
MacBook ever.

00:00:22.610 --> 00:00:26.340
Let’s discuss everything that’s great
and not-so-great about the new base model

00:00:26.340 --> 00:00:27.340
MacBook Pro.

00:00:27.340 --> 00:00:32.020
For starters, Apple was able to equip it with
the Touch Bar at the base price, something

00:00:32.020 --> 00:00:34.640
you previously had to pay at least $1800 to
get.

00:00:34.640 --> 00:00:38.630
Now of course, not everybody likes it, but
what really matters is the hardware that comes

00:00:38.630 --> 00:00:43.580
with it, like Touch ID authentication, which
makes it really convenient to login and install

00:00:43.580 --> 00:00:46.130
new apps without having to reenter your password.

00:00:46.130 --> 00:00:50.720
But the major deal is Apple’s T2 chip, which
takes care of a bunch of system controllers

00:00:50.720 --> 00:00:52.440
and makes it more secure.

00:00:52.440 --> 00:00:57.059
And on top of that, it brings hardware HEVC
video transcoding, and many people believe

00:00:57.059 --> 00:01:02.320
that HEVC is going to be the future mainstream
codec, so having the T2 chip makes this MacBook

00:01:02.320 --> 00:01:04.430
Pro much more future-proof.

00:01:04.430 --> 00:01:08.080
Design wise, almost everything else about
the new base MacBook Pro basically matches

00:01:08.080 --> 00:01:12.330
the more expensive MacBook Pros, like the
very color accurate and bright display with

00:01:12.330 --> 00:01:17.600
True Tone technology, the class-leading force
touch trackpad, and the all-aluminum lightweight

00:01:17.600 --> 00:01:19.290
and incredibly thin design.

00:01:19.290 --> 00:01:23.450
Along with that, we also have the latest version
of Apple’s butterfly keyboard, similar to

00:01:23.450 --> 00:01:27.250
the 3rd gen in 2018 but with updated materials.

00:01:27.250 --> 00:01:32.299
This alone was a big reason why some people
would spend more for the 2018 $1800 model

00:01:32.299 --> 00:01:34.049
or go with a MacBook Air instead.

00:01:34.049 --> 00:01:38.970
The previous Base macbook Pro had a keyboard
design back from 2016, which was the most

00:01:38.970 --> 00:01:39.990
prone to failure.

00:01:39.990 --> 00:01:43.930
We’re not sure if this latest design will
solve all the problems just yet, but if any

00:01:43.930 --> 00:01:46.691
issues arise, Apple will fix them for free.

00:01:46.691 --> 00:01:50.820
What really makes this years’ base MacBook
Pro standout is the new quad-core processor,

00:01:50.820 --> 00:01:54.619
and from the first glance on Apple’s website,
it seems like it’s quite a bit slower than

00:01:54.619 --> 00:02:00.440
the $1800 MacBook Pro’s 2.4GHz processor,
but that couldn’t be further from the truth.

00:02:00.440 --> 00:02:04.560
We made a full video comparing both models
and we found that under load, the base MacBook

00:02:04.560 --> 00:02:11.940
Pro ran only 300 MHz slower than the $1800
model, nowhere near the 1Ghz, or 1000MHz difference

00:02:11.940 --> 00:02:13.220
we see on the spec sheet.

00:02:13.220 --> 00:02:18.180
We ran a ton of benchmarks, video editing
tests and even some photo editing, and found

00:02:18.180 --> 00:02:25.200
that the $1800 MacBook Pro is only around
5-9% faster than the $1300 model, so for performance

00:02:25.200 --> 00:02:27.730
alone, it definitely wasn’t worth the upgrade.

00:02:27.730 --> 00:02:32.020
If you’re looking to do video editing on
a budget, we were able to play back full resolution

00:02:32.020 --> 00:02:39.340
4K H.264 and H.265 Files without any dropped
frames in both Premiere Pro and Final Cut,

00:02:39.340 --> 00:02:43.739
and even adding color correction and a Lut
would be perfectly fine using Final Cut, while

00:02:43.739 --> 00:02:48.140
Premiere would require you to drop down to
half resolution, which is still very usable.

00:02:48.140 --> 00:02:52.560
But that’s not the case for editing in Davinci
Resolve, which likes to have more video memory

00:02:52.560 --> 00:02:55.069
like on the 15” with dedicated graphics.

00:02:55.069 --> 00:02:59.650
Overall, we’re impressed how this MacBook
performs, but keep in mind that final exports

00:02:59.650 --> 00:03:02.810
will require some patience compared to 15”
MacBook Pros.

00:03:02.810 --> 00:03:07.840
About 2-3 times longer than your project length
in Final Cut Pro, or up to 10 times longer

00:03:07.840 --> 00:03:10.879
in Premiere, so personally we’d stick with
final cut.

00:03:10.879 --> 00:03:15.040
For most people that do simple 4K editing
from time to time this new MacBook Pro will

00:03:15.040 --> 00:03:19.370
do a surprisingly good job, absolutely smoking
the previous model thanks to the quad core

00:03:19.370 --> 00:03:21.209
CPU and updated graphics.

00:03:21.209 --> 00:03:25.100
The same thing goes for Photo editing, but
here the performance difference between the

00:03:25.100 --> 00:03:29.349
13” and 15” models are even lower since
graphics are hardly used.

00:03:29.349 --> 00:03:35.350
Importing 50 42MP RAW images into Lightroom
classic was very quick, and exporting edited

00:03:35.350 --> 00:03:37.130
Jpegs was also impressive.

00:03:37.130 --> 00:03:41.569
We did notice a small delay in some tasks
that we don’t get on our 13” MacBook with

00:03:41.569 --> 00:03:47.180
16GB of RAM, which makes sense since the 8GB
are maxed out while editing photos.

00:03:47.180 --> 00:03:51.970
Upgrading to 16GB of RAM will not only smooth
out your editing experience but speed up exporting

00:03:51.970 --> 00:03:52.970
as well.

00:03:52.970 --> 00:03:58.650
If you’re doing 12-24 MP images in small
batches, 8GB will perform well enough, but

00:03:58.650 --> 00:04:02.670
if you are working with higher resolution
files or doing things like HDR, Panoramas,

00:04:02.670 --> 00:04:07.959
or working with hundreds of images, we would
highly recommend spending $200 more and upgrading

00:04:07.959 --> 00:04:09.519
to 16GB.

00:04:09.519 --> 00:04:14.580
We also decided to include some programming
tests, but unfortunately, us being video editors

00:04:14.580 --> 00:04:19.249
with absolutely no xCode experience at all,
we weren’t able to find and run a sample

00:04:19.249 --> 00:04:22.930
xCode project that would be easy to replicate
on future machines.

00:04:22.930 --> 00:04:27.529
So if you guys know of a sample xCode project
we could use, comment below, but from what

00:04:27.529 --> 00:04:31.499
we’ve seen so far with this machine, you’re
getting truly impressive processor performance

00:04:31.499 --> 00:04:34.490
for the cash out of this 1.4GHz CPU.

00:04:34.490 --> 00:04:38.119
And we think it’s gonna perform much better
than it’s Windows rivals and completely

00:04:38.119 --> 00:04:40.729
blow the 2019 MacBook Air out of the water.

00:04:40.729 --> 00:04:43.930
We’ll be getting our hands on that soon
and doing a full comparison, so make sure

00:04:43.930 --> 00:04:45.270
to subscribe now for that!

00:04:45.270 --> 00:04:47.539
And now let’s talk about the downsides.

00:04:47.539 --> 00:04:51.310
When we first got this laptop, we decided
to take off the bottom cover and compare the

00:04:51.310 --> 00:04:56.240
internals to the $1800 MacBook Pro, and we
found a few interesting details.

00:04:56.240 --> 00:04:59.850
First off, we’re only getting a single fan
in the base model compared to two on the more

00:04:59.850 --> 00:05:04.879
expensive Pro, but seeing as we got such impressive
performance results, with CPU temps that were

00:05:04.879 --> 00:05:09.419
only slightly hotter than the $1800 model,
that doesn’t seem to be a problem.

00:05:09.419 --> 00:05:13.610
We also noticed that the speakers look way,
way different, and when we tested them, we

00:05:13.610 --> 00:05:17.159
noticed a huge improvement on the more expensive
13” Pro.

00:05:17.159 --> 00:05:35.680
Just listen for yourself.

00:05:35.680 --> 00:05:59.059
And then we tested the SSD storage speed,
and found that the 128GB SSD was quite a bit

00:05:59.059 --> 00:06:05.210
slower than the 256gig on the $1800 model,
and not only that, but if you pay the extra

00:06:05.210 --> 00:06:12.219
$200 to get the 256GB SSD, it’s actually
still slower than the 256gig on the $1800

00:06:12.219 --> 00:06:13.219
Pro.

00:06:13.219 --> 00:06:17.300
So there are a couple of downsides already,
but the biggest difference is that the $1300

00:06:17.300 --> 00:06:21.699
model only gets two Thunderbolt 3 ports, while
the more expensive one gets four of them.

00:06:21.699 --> 00:06:26.199
So we can see that while the new base MacBook
Pro offers incredible value for performance,

00:06:26.199 --> 00:06:29.520
there are a few things that you have to sacrifice,
but let’s take a look at all of these things

00:06:29.520 --> 00:06:32.509
from my own personal opinion and past experiences.

00:06:32.509 --> 00:06:36.550
As far as performance goes, I think this machine
is the way to go if you don’t do professional

00:06:36.550 --> 00:06:41.560
work every day or if you’re doing entry-level
basic stuff, like editing youtube videos.

00:06:41.560 --> 00:06:45.159
Or if you’re doing your work every single
day or if your business depends on it, like

00:06:45.159 --> 00:06:49.619
editing wedding videos, then I would honestly
recommend just jumping to the 15” model

00:06:49.619 --> 00:06:50.990
with dedicated graphics.

00:06:50.990 --> 00:06:55.360
If you’re doing a lot of photo editing or
programming, I think just getting 16GB of

00:06:55.360 --> 00:06:58.259
RAM on this model will be a great choice for
almost everyone.

00:06:58.259 --> 00:07:01.949
And for the single fan, we didn’t really
notice higher temps at all.

00:07:01.949 --> 00:07:05.639
One difference we noticed between this and
the dual-fan model was that the single fan

00:07:05.639 --> 00:07:09.939
would keep blowing at max fan speed during
longer tasks like exporting, while the dual-fan

00:07:09.939 --> 00:07:14.089
would eventually slow down a bit after the
temps came down, but as far as performance

00:07:14.089 --> 00:07:16.490
itself, the single fan is completely fine.

00:07:16.490 --> 00:07:20.899
Now for the speakers, I personally like to
watch youtube videos, shows and movies on

00:07:20.899 --> 00:07:24.779
my MacBook, so that’s actually the most
important thing for myself, personally.

00:07:24.779 --> 00:07:28.449
Now if you prefer to use headphones and you
like to watch youtube videos on your phone

00:07:28.449 --> 00:07:32.669
and shows on your TV, then the speaker difference
won’t really matter much to you.

00:07:32.669 --> 00:07:37.399
And in reality, the $1300 models speakers
are still better than practically any Windows

00:07:37.399 --> 00:07:38.399
laptop out there.

00:07:38.399 --> 00:07:43.919
As for the SSD speed difference, even the
slower speeds of the 128GB are nearly as fast

00:07:43.919 --> 00:07:48.410
as a lot of the premium laptops out there,
like Dell’s XPS, so it’ll be fine for

00:07:48.410 --> 00:07:52.210
most people, so you should really just worry
about the storage amount itself unless you

00:07:52.210 --> 00:07:53.759
know you need faster speeds.

00:07:53.759 --> 00:07:58.330
And finally, I think the factory 8GB of RAM
will be okay for a lot of people, but I personally

00:07:58.330 --> 00:08:02.339
love to keep a lot of Chrome tabs open, so
if you’re like me, you’ll be much better

00:08:02.339 --> 00:08:06.869
off with 16GB, and especially if you’re
doing productivity tasks more than once or

00:08:06.869 --> 00:08:07.869
twice a week.

00:08:07.869 --> 00:08:11.349
For the ports, I’ve found that I don’t
really use them that often, but even then,

00:08:11.349 --> 00:08:14.899
it’s really convenient to be able to swap
the charger to the other side, so it’s a

00:08:14.899 --> 00:08:19.539
lot more convenient, and I also like to charge
my phone using a USB-C to Lightning cable,

00:08:19.539 --> 00:08:22.219
so that’s already maxing out two ports.

00:08:22.219 --> 00:08:27.650
So if I needed to transfer some files, I’d
need to unplug one of them if I only had two

00:08:27.650 --> 00:08:28.650
ports.

00:08:28.650 --> 00:08:31.110
So if you’re planning on actively using
the Thunderbolt 3 ports like for connecting

00:08:31.110 --> 00:08:36.219
an external display or working off of an external
SSD, you probably would be better off going

00:08:36.219 --> 00:08:38.870
for the $1800 model with four ports.

00:08:38.870 --> 00:08:43.099
Now you could make it work with a Thunderbolt
3 hub off Amazon, but it’s not as convenient

00:08:43.099 --> 00:08:44.329
to carry that around.

00:08:44.329 --> 00:08:48.060
But if you’re a simple person who only needs
those ports for charging and occasionally

00:08:48.060 --> 00:08:52.279
connecting an external drive for some quick
file transfers, then you should honestly just

00:08:52.279 --> 00:08:53.850
be fine with 2 ports.

00:08:53.850 --> 00:08:57.250
One last question we want to address that
some of you may be asking is if its worth

00:08:57.250 --> 00:09:02.779
buying a discounted 2018 4-port model instead
of a lower end 2019 config.

00:09:02.779 --> 00:09:08.430
The 2018 versions perform almost as good as
the 2019’s in terms of CPU, but they do

00:09:08.430 --> 00:09:10.620
have keyboards that have an older design.

00:09:10.620 --> 00:09:17.629
Personally, if you were going to buy a $1500
256GB 2019 MacBook Pro and can score an older

00:09:17.629 --> 00:09:21.829
4 thunderbolt model for up to $100 more we
would make the jump since you also get better

00:09:21.829 --> 00:09:27.519
speakers and faster SSD’s, but only if you
can get 16GB of RAM on the 2018 model.

00:09:27.519 --> 00:09:31.620
All in all, if you want to get access to macOS
and not have to worry about performance issues

00:09:31.620 --> 00:09:37.879
in the future, I think the 2019 base MacBook
Pro for $1300 is the best value MacBook in

00:09:37.879 --> 00:09:40.610
years, and I think almost everyone will be
happy with it.

00:09:40.610 --> 00:09:45.050
If you enjoyed this review, make sure to tap
like and click the circle above to subscribe

00:09:45.050 --> 00:09:47.000
so you don’t miss out on more videos like
this one.

00:09:47.000 --> 00:10:01.050
Thanks for watching, and we’ll see you in
the next one!

